-- Migration: Bloom's L3 rebalancing — FAR batch 4 top-up (10 questions)
-- Date: 2026-02-21
-- Purpose: Rewrite 10 additional L2 questions to L3 (Analysis) depth to push FAR from 24% to 25%+ L3
-- Affected topics: Revenue Recognition, Consolidations, Income Taxes, Fixed Assets, Governmental Accounting, Equity, Leases, Fair Value, Financial Statements, Bonds and Debt

BEGIN;

-- ============================================================
-- REVENUE RECOGNITION (1 question)
-- ============================================================

-- ID 34: Revenue Recognition — Five-step model: effect of skipping Step 2 on revenue timing
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Meridian Software sells a $240,000 bundled contract that includes a perpetual software license and three years of post-contract support (PCS). The standalone selling prices are $180,000 for the license and $90,000 for PCS ($30,000/year). Meridian''s controller allocates $160,000 to the license and $80,000 to PCS using relative standalone selling prices. If the controller had failed to identify the PCS as a separate performance obligation (skipping Step 2 of the five-step model), all $240,000 would be recognized at license delivery. How does failing to identify separate performance obligations affect the timing and amount of revenue recognized in Year 1?',
  choices = '["Failing to separate performance obligations has no effect because the total contract revenue of $240,000 is the same regardless of how many obligations are identified", "Correctly separating the obligations results in Year 1 revenue of $186,667 ($160,000 license + $26,667 first-year PCS), while failing to separate results in $240,000 at delivery — the $53,333 overstatement in Year 1 accelerates revenue recognition and violates ASC 606 by ignoring that PCS transfers over time, distorting both the current period income statement and the pattern of future revenue", "Failing to separate the obligations understates Year 1 revenue because the entire contract would be deferred until PCS delivery is complete", "The five-step model does not require identification of separate performance obligations when the contract has a single customer"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under ASC 606-10-25-14, Step 2 requires identifying each distinct performance obligation in the contract. The license transfers at a point in time, while PCS transfers over three years. Proper allocation: Year 1 = $160,000 (license at delivery) + $26,667 (one year of $80,000 PCS) = $186,667. Without Step 2, the controller treats the entire bundle as one obligation, recognizing $240,000 at delivery — overstating Year 1 revenue by $53,333 and leaving no revenue in Years 2-3 for PCS. This distorts the matching of revenue with the ongoing service delivery. (A) is wrong because while total contract revenue is identical, the timing of recognition materially affects each period''s reported results. (C) reverses the effect — failing to separate accelerates recognition, not defers it. (D) is wrong because ASC 606 requires Step 2 for all contracts regardless of customer count.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 34;

-- ============================================================
-- CONSOLIDATIONS (1 question)
-- ============================================================

-- ID 55: Consolidations — NCI measurement effect on goodwill and post-acquisition income allocation
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Thornfield Corp. acquires 80% of Summit Industries for $8 million when Summit''s identifiable net assets have a fair value of $9 million. If Thornfield measures the 20% noncontrolling interest (NCI) at fair value of $2.1 million, total goodwill is $1.1 million. If Thornfield instead measures NCI at its proportionate share of identifiable net assets ($1.8 million), goodwill is $800,000. In Year 1 after acquisition, Summit reports net income of $600,000. How does the NCI measurement choice affect both the goodwill amount and the allocation of post-acquisition income in the consolidated financial statements?',
  choices = '["The NCI measurement choice has no effect on post-acquisition income allocation — NCI always receives 20% of Summit''s net income ($120,000) regardless of the initial measurement method", "Full fair value NCI produces $300,000 more goodwill and allocates more income to the parent because the higher goodwill base reduces NCI''s share of subsidiary earnings", "Full fair value NCI produces $300,000 more goodwill ($1.1M vs $800K) reflecting goodwill attributable to both the parent and NCI — the income allocation is identical under both methods ($480,000 to parent, $120,000 to NCI) because ASC 810 allocates subsidiary income based on ownership percentage, not on how NCI was initially measured — but the higher goodwill under the fair value method means a larger asset is subject to impairment testing, which could produce different future income effects", "The proportionate share method is prohibited under U.S. GAAP — only the fair value method is permitted for NCI measurement"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): Under ASC 805-20-30, NCI can be measured at either fair value or proportionate share of identifiable net assets. Full fair value: goodwill = ($8M + $2.1M) - $9M = $1.1M. Proportionate: goodwill = $8M - (80% x $9M) = $8M - $7.2M = $800K. The $300,000 difference represents goodwill attributable to the NCI. Post-acquisition, ASC 810-10-45-15 allocates subsidiary income based on ownership percentage regardless of initial NCI measurement: parent gets 80% ($480,000), NCI gets 20% ($120,000). However, the different goodwill amounts affect future impairment testing under ASC 350 — the $1.1M goodwill has a higher carrying amount subject to write-down than the $800K. (A) is partially correct about income allocation but ignores the goodwill and impairment implications. (B) is wrong because the NCI method does not affect income allocation percentages. (D) is wrong because both methods are permitted under U.S. GAAP.',
  correct_index = 2
WHERE id = 55;

-- ============================================================
-- INCOME TAXES (1 question)
-- ============================================================

-- ID 49: Income Taxes — Temporary difference direction and its effect on deferred tax balance sheet classification
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Prescott Manufacturing has two significant temporary differences at December 31, Year 1. First, accelerated tax depreciation created a $400,000 excess of tax depreciation over book depreciation (the asset''s tax basis is $400,000 lower than its book basis). Second, a $150,000 warranty accrual is recorded for books but not yet deductible for tax purposes (the liability''s tax basis is zero while the book basis is $150,000). The enacted tax rate is 25%. Analyze how each temporary difference creates a deferred tax asset or liability, and what happens to each balance when it reverses in future periods.',
  choices = '["Both differences create deferred tax liabilities because both will increase taxable income in future years", "The depreciation difference creates a $100,000 deferred tax liability (future taxable amounts as book depreciation exceeds tax depreciation) and the warranty difference creates a $37,500 deferred tax asset (future deductible amounts when warranties are paid) — the DTL reverses as future book depreciation exceeds declining tax depreciation, increasing tax payments, while the DTA reverses when warranty claims are paid and become tax-deductible, reducing tax payments — the net effect is a $62,500 net DTL on the balance sheet", "Both differences create deferred tax assets because both reduce the company''s current tax obligation", "The depreciation difference creates a $37,500 deferred tax asset and the warranty difference creates a $100,000 deferred tax liability, producing a net $62,500 DTL"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under ASC 740-10-25, deferred tax consequences follow the future reversal pattern. The depreciation difference: the asset''s tax basis ($400K lower) means future tax depreciation will be less than book depreciation, creating future taxable amounts — a DTL of $400,000 x 25% = $100,000. This reverses as the company pays more tax in future years when book depreciation exceeds tax depreciation. The warranty accrual: the $150,000 book expense is not yet tax-deductible, creating future deductible amounts when claims are paid — a DTA of $150,000 x 25% = $37,500. This reverses when warranty payments generate tax deductions. Net: $100,000 DTL - $37,500 DTA = $62,500 net DTL. (A) is wrong because the warranty creates future deductible amounts (DTA), not taxable amounts. (C) is wrong because the depreciation timing difference creates future taxable amounts (DTL). (D) reverses the classification of both differences.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 49;

-- ============================================================
-- FIXED ASSETS (1 question)
-- ============================================================

-- ID 39: Fixed Assets — Depreciation method effect on expense pattern and asset turnover comparability
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Ridgemont Logistics and Cascade Freight both purchase identical delivery trucks for $200,000 with 10-year useful lives and $20,000 residual values. Ridgemont uses straight-line depreciation while Cascade uses double-declining-balance. After Year 3, both companies have generated identical cumulative revenue of $900,000 from their trucks. How does the depreciation method choice affect each company''s Year 3 depreciation expense, accumulated depreciation, and fixed asset turnover ratio, and what does the divergence reveal about cross-company comparability?',
  choices = '["Both methods produce identical Year 3 expense and accumulated depreciation because they use the same cost, life, and residual value", "Ridgemont (SL) records $18,000 Year 3 expense with accumulated depreciation of $54,000 (NBV $146,000), while Cascade (DDB) records $25,600 Year 3 expense with accumulated depreciation of $109,600 (NBV $90,400) — Cascade''s lower net book value produces a higher fixed asset turnover ratio ($900K/$90.4K = 10.0 vs $900K/$146K = 6.2), creating the misleading appearance of greater capital efficiency when in reality the difference is driven entirely by the depreciation method, not operational performance", "Cascade''s accelerated method produces lower accumulated depreciation than Ridgemont''s straight-line method because DDB uses a declining base", "The depreciation method has no effect on the fixed asset turnover ratio because both companies have the same original cost"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Straight-line: ($200,000 - $20,000) / 10 = $18,000/year. Year 3 accumulated = $54,000, NBV = $146,000. DDB rate = 2/10 = 20%. Year 1: $200,000 x 20% = $40,000. Year 2: $160,000 x 20% = $32,000. Year 3: $128,000 x 20% = $25,600. Accumulated = $97,600, NBV = $102,400. Fixed asset turnover = revenue / net fixed assets. Ridgemont: $900K / $146K = 6.2. Cascade: $900K / $102.4K = 8.8. The ratio divergence is entirely methodological — both companies operate identical assets generating identical revenue, yet Cascade appears 42% more capital-efficient. This illustrates why analysts must normalize for depreciation methods when comparing asset utilization across companies. (A) is wrong because the two methods produce materially different expense patterns. (C) reverses the relationship — DDB front-loads depreciation, producing higher accumulated depreciation in early years. (D) is wrong because the ratio uses net book value, which differs by method.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 39;

-- ============================================================
-- GOVERNMENTAL ACCOUNTING (1 question)
-- ============================================================

-- ID 52: Governmental Accounting — Restricted grant fund classification and its effect on fund vs government-wide reporting
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'The City of Ashford receives a $2 million state grant restricted for road construction. The public works director asks whether the grant should be recorded in the general fund or a capital projects fund. The finance director notes that the choice affects how the transaction appears in both fund-level and government-wide financial statements. How does the fund classification decision affect the reporting of this grant, and why does the dual reporting model produce different measurements of the same transaction?',
  choices = '["The grant must be recorded in the general fund regardless of restrictions, and the government-wide statements will report it identically because both use the same measurement focus", "Recording the grant in a capital projects fund segregates it from general operations at the fund level, clearly tracking compliance with the restriction — in the fund-level statements (modified accrual), the grant is recognized as revenue when measurable and available, and expenditures are recognized when the liability is incurred — in the government-wide statements (full accrual), the same grant is revenue when eligibility requirements are met, and the road construction is capitalized as infrastructure rather than expensed, creating a fundamental difference in how the same dollars appear across the two reporting layers", "The fund classification has no effect because all governmental funds are consolidated into a single column in the government-wide statements", "The grant can only be reported in a special revenue fund because it is restricted for a specific purpose, and both fund-level and government-wide statements report it as an operating expense"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under GASB 34, the capital projects fund is the appropriate fund for tracking resources restricted for capital asset acquisition or construction. At the fund level (modified accrual, current financial resources measurement focus), the $2 million flows through as revenue when available and construction costs are reported as expenditures when incurred — no asset is capitalized. At the government-wide level (full accrual, economic resources measurement focus), the same transaction is reported as program revenue in the governmental activities column, and the road is capitalized as infrastructure and depreciated (unless the modified approach is used). This dual reporting illustrates GASB 34''s design: fund statements show fiscal compliance and near-term resource flows, while government-wide statements show long-term economic position. (A) is wrong because the general fund typically does not track restricted capital grants. (C) oversimplifies — governmental funds are combined, not consolidated, and the measurement focus differs. (D) is wrong because capital projects funds, not special revenue funds, are used for capital asset construction.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 52;

-- ============================================================
-- EQUITY (1 question)
-- ============================================================

-- ID 47: Equity — Small stock dividend vs large stock dividend: differential effect on equity components
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Waverly Corp. has 100,000 shares of $5 par common stock outstanding with a market price of $40 per share. Retained earnings is $2,000,000 and additional paid-in capital is $800,000. The board considers two alternatives: a 10% stock dividend (small) or a 30% stock dividend (large). How does the size of the stock dividend affect the amounts transferred between equity components, and why does GAAP require different treatment for small versus large dividends?',
  choices = '["Both dividends transfer the same total amount from retained earnings — the only difference is the number of shares issued, which has no effect on equity component balances", "The 10% small dividend transfers $400,000 from retained earnings (10,000 shares x $40 market value) — $50,000 to common stock and $350,000 to APIC — while the 30% large dividend transfers only $150,000 from retained earnings (30,000 shares x $5 par value) to common stock with no APIC impact — the small dividend uses market value because investors perceive small dividends as income distributions, while the large dividend uses par value because it is essentially a stock split, and using market value would inappropriately deplete retained earnings for what is economically a recapitalization", "Both dividends must use market value because ASC 505 requires fair value measurement for all equity transactions", "The large dividend transfers more from retained earnings because more shares are issued, while the small dividend transfers less"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under ASC 505-20-30, small stock dividends (generally below 20-25%) are recorded at the market value of the additional shares: 10,000 shares x $40 = $400,000. Retained earnings decreases by $400,000, common stock increases by $50,000 (10,000 x $5 par), and APIC increases by $350,000. Large stock dividends (above 20-25%) are recorded at par value only: 30,000 shares x $5 = $150,000. Retained earnings decreases by $150,000 and common stock increases by $150,000. The rationale: small dividends are perceived by the market as income-like distributions, justifying market value capitalization. Large dividends are economically equivalent to stock splits — no per-share value change is expected — so only the minimum legal requirement (par value) transfers. Using market value for a 30% dividend would deplete $1.2 million from retained earnings for what is essentially a recapitalization. (A) is wrong because the valuation basis differs significantly. (C) is wrong because large dividends use par value. (D) reverses the relationship.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 47;

-- ============================================================
-- LEASES (1 question)
-- ============================================================

-- ID 44: Leases — Finance vs operating lease: effect on expense pattern and leverage ratios
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Clearwater Corp. enters a 5-year lease for office equipment with annual payments of $50,000 paid at year-end. The implicit rate is 6%, producing a present value of $210,618. Under ASC 842, if the lease is classified as a finance lease, Year 1 total expense is $62,637 (depreciation of $42,124 + interest of $12,637 minus nothing). If classified as an operating lease, Year 1 total expense is $50,000 (straight-line). How does the lease classification affect the pattern of total expense over the 5-year term and the company''s debt-to-equity ratio at inception?',
  choices = '["Both classifications produce identical total expense each year and identical balance sheet effects — the classification distinction is eliminated under ASC 842", "Finance lease classification front-loads total expense (Year 1: $54,761 vs $50,000 operating) because the interest component is highest in early years when the liability balance is largest, while straight-line operating lease expense remains constant — both classifications increase liabilities by $210,618 at inception (recognizing the lease liability), producing the same debt-to-equity impact at inception, but the finance lease''s faster amortization reduces the ROU asset more quickly, creating divergent asset-to-equity ratios over the lease term", "Operating lease classification produces higher total expense over the lease term because the straight-line method ignores the time value of money", "Finance lease classification has no balance sheet effect because the payments are expensed as incurred, similar to the pre-ASC 842 operating lease treatment"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under ASC 842, both lease types recognize a right-of-use asset and lease liability at inception (PV of $210,618), producing identical initial balance sheet leverage effects. The expense pattern diverges: finance leases bifurcate expense into depreciation (straight-line ROU amortization = $210,618/5 = $42,124/year) and interest (declining as liability is repaid: Year 1 = $210,618 x 6% = $12,637). Total finance lease expense in Year 1 = $54,761, declining over the term. Operating lease expense is straight-line at $50,000/year. Total 5-year expense is identical ($250,000 = 5 x $50,000), but the timing differs — finance leases are front-loaded. The ROU asset also declines faster under finance leases because the amortization pattern differs from the liability reduction pattern. (A) is wrong because annual expense amounts differ despite equal totals. (C) is wrong because total expense is equal across both methods. (D) is wrong because ASC 842 requires balance sheet recognition for both types.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 44;

-- ============================================================
-- FAIR VALUE (1 question)
-- ============================================================

-- ID 1148: Fair Value — Mixed hierarchy inputs: classification effect on disclosure requirements and audit scrutiny
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Thornfield Investments holds a mortgage-backed security valued at $5.2 million. The valuation uses observable yield curves from active markets (Level 2 input) for discounting, but relies on a management-developed prepayment speed assumption (Level 3 input) that significantly affects the present value calculation. A 1% change in the prepayment assumption shifts the valuation by $380,000. Under ASC 820, how should this instrument be classified within the fair value hierarchy, and what are the implications of using a significant Level 3 input alongside observable data?',
  choices = '["The instrument is classified as Level 2 because the observable yield curve is the primary input and the prepayment assumption is secondary", "The instrument is classified at the lowest level of any significant input — Level 3 — because the management-developed prepayment assumption materially affects the valuation ($380,000 sensitivity) despite the presence of observable yield curves — this triggers enhanced disclosure requirements including a roll-forward reconciliation of beginning to ending balances, description of the valuation process, and quantitative sensitivity analysis, and subjects the valuation to heightened audit scrutiny because the unobservable input introduces estimation uncertainty that cannot be independently verified", "The instrument is classified at the average of its inputs — Level 2.5 — because it uses a mix of observable and unobservable data", "The instrument should be split into two components: the yield curve portion at Level 2 and the prepayment portion at Level 3, with separate fair values reported for each"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under ASC 820-10-35-37, when a fair value measurement uses inputs from multiple hierarchy levels, the entire measurement is classified at the lowest level of any significant input. The prepayment speed assumption is unobservable (Level 3) and significant — a 1% change produces a $380,000 swing (7.3% of the $5.2M value). Therefore, the entire instrument is Level 3 despite using Level 2 yield curves. ASC 820-10-50-2 requires Level 3 measurements to disclose: (1) a tabular roll-forward, (2) valuation techniques and inputs used, (3) the effect of changes in unobservable inputs on fair value, and (4) quantitative sensitivity information. Auditors must evaluate the reasonableness of management''s prepayment assumption and test the model''s sensitivity, increasing audit effort. (A) is wrong because classification is based on the lowest significant input, not the primary input. (C) is wrong because the hierarchy does not use fractional levels. (D) is wrong because the instrument is classified as a single unit, not split by input.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 1148;

-- ============================================================
-- FINANCIAL STATEMENTS (1 question)
-- ============================================================

-- ID 147: Financial Statements — Balance sheet equation: effect of error on contributed capital determination
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Waverly Holdings reports total assets of $800,000, total liabilities of $500,000, and retained earnings of $150,000 on its December 31 balance sheet. An analyst uses these figures to derive contributed capital (total equity minus retained earnings). Subsequently, the analyst discovers that a $40,000 prepaid insurance asset was misclassified as an expense, understating both total assets and net income (and therefore retained earnings) by $40,000 before tax. The tax rate is 25%. How does correcting this error affect the balance sheet equation components and the derived contributed capital, and what does this reveal about the interdependence of the financial statements?',
  choices = '["Correcting the error increases total assets by $40,000 and retained earnings by $40,000, leaving contributed capital unchanged — the error affects only the asset and equity sides symmetrically", "Correcting the error increases total assets by $40,000, increases the deferred tax liability by $10,000 (tax effect), and increases retained earnings by $30,000 (after-tax) — contributed capital (previously calculated as $150,000) is actually unchanged at $150,000 because the error affected only retained earnings and a tax liability, not contributed capital — this demonstrates that errors flowing through the income statement affect retained earnings and potentially liabilities but do not alter the equity contributed by shareholders", "Correcting the error has no balance sheet effect because insurance expense is a period cost that does not affect the balance sheet", "Correcting the error reduces contributed capital by $30,000 because the retained earnings increase must be offset by a contributed capital decrease to keep total equity unchanged"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Original equity = $800,000 - $500,000 = $300,000. Contributed capital = $300,000 - $150,000 = $150,000. After correction: assets increase by $40,000 (prepaid insurance recognized), a deferred tax liability of $10,000 is created (the temporary difference between book basis of $40,000 and tax basis of $0 at 25%), and retained earnings increase by $30,000 (the after-tax effect of reversing the incorrect expense). Corrected totals: assets $840,000, liabilities $510,000, equity $330,000, retained earnings $180,000, contributed capital $150,000 (unchanged). The error chain illustrates financial statement interdependence: a balance sheet misclassification (prepaid as expense) flows through the income statement (overstated expense), reduces retained earnings, and understates both assets and equity. Contributed capital is unaffected because it reflects shareholder contributions, not operating results. (A) ignores the tax effect. (C) is wrong because the prepaid is a balance sheet asset. (D) is wrong because total equity increases by $30,000 — it is not held constant.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 147;

-- ============================================================
-- BONDS AND DEBT (1 question)
-- ============================================================

-- ID 169: Bonds and Debt — Early extinguishment gain/loss: carrying amount vs reacquisition price analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Greystone Corp. issued $2 million of 8% bonds at 105 five years ago. The premium has been amortized using the effective interest method, and the current carrying amount is $2,048,000. Greystone purchases the bonds on the open market for 98 ($1,960,000) to extinguish them early. Simultaneously, Ashford Industries retires its own $2 million bonds (originally issued at 95) with a current carrying amount of $1,970,000 by purchasing them at 101 ($2,020,000). Compare the gain/loss recognized by each company and explain why the direction of the gain or loss depends on the relationship between carrying amount and reacquisition price.',
  choices = '["Both companies recognize a loss because early extinguishment always results in a penalty cost above face value", "Greystone recognizes an $88,000 gain ($2,048,000 carrying - $1,960,000 reacquisition) because its premium-issued bonds have a carrying amount above the repurchase price, while Ashford recognizes a $50,000 loss ($2,020,000 reacquisition - $1,970,000 carrying) because its discount-issued bonds have a carrying amount below the repurchase price — the gain or loss under ASC 470-50 reflects whether the company paid more or less than its book obligation, which is independent of the original issuance price or face value", "Both companies recognize a gain because they are eliminating future interest obligations", "Greystone recognizes a $40,000 gain (face value minus reacquisition price) and Ashford recognizes a $20,000 loss (reacquisition price minus face value) — gains and losses are always measured against face value"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under ASC 470-50-40, the gain or loss on early extinguishment equals the difference between the carrying amount and the reacquisition price. Greystone: carrying $2,048,000 > reacquisition $1,960,000 = $88,000 gain. The premium-amortized bonds still carry above face value, and market conditions allowed repurchase below that carrying amount. Ashford: reacquisition $2,020,000 > carrying $1,970,000 = $50,000 loss. The discount-issued bonds carry below face value, and Ashford paid above the carrying amount to retire them. The comparison demonstrates that the gain or loss is purely a function of carrying amount vs. cash paid — not face value, not original issuance price. (A) is wrong because extinguishment can produce either gains or losses depending on market conditions. (C) is wrong because eliminating future interest does not generate an accounting gain — the gain/loss is a current-period measurement. (D) is wrong because face value is irrelevant; carrying amount (after amortization) is the measurement basis.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 169;

COMMIT;
